  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
